40763144,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence provided,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40763144,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma or dried blood spot (DBS),A Plasma or dried blood spot (DBS) sample is collected and referred to the HIVDR testing facility at a central national laboratory.,The paper mentions that plasma or DBS samples were collected for analysis.
40763144,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"All PLHIV, regardless of age, who had been on ART for at least one year, had a non-suppressed HIV viral load (>1000 copies/ml) and underwent an HIVDR test between 1st January 2021 – 30th December 2023.",The study focused on individuals with virological failure.
40763144,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,We conducted a retrospective cross-sectional analysis of HIVDR data in the West-Nile region of Uganda across the 161 health facilities that offer comprehensive Anti-retroviral therapy (ART) services.,The paper does not mention any clinical trials involving the patients.
40763144,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,295,"During the study period, 363 PLHIV had virological failure but only 295 (81.3%) had HIV DR results and were considered for the study.",The paper states that 295 individuals had HIV drug resistance results.
40763144,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"All PLHIV, regardless of age, who had been on ART for at least one year, had a non-suppressed HIV viral load (>1000 copies/ml) and underwent an HIVDR test between 1st January 2021 – 30th December 2023.",The study included individuals who had been on ART for at least one year.
40763144,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI",Majority of participants (65.4%) underwent HIVDR testing in 2023 following revised national criteria to test all PLHIV failing on ART regardless of treatment line (Table 1).,The paper mentions that the study included participants on various ART regimens.
40763144,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"TDF/3TC/DTG, AZT/3TC/DTG, ABC/3TC/DTG, ABC/3TC/LPVr, TDF/3TC/ATVr, AZT/3TC/ATVr, OTHERS","All PLHIV, regardless of age, who had been on ART for at least one year, had a non-suppressed HIV viral load (>1000 copies/ml) and underwent an HIVDR test between 1st January 2021–30th December 2023. During the study period, 363 PLHIV had virological failure but only 295 (81.3%) had HIV DR results and were considered for the study.",The paper lists multiple drug regimens in use among the participants.
40763144,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,The data used in this study is available on the Ugandan Ministry of Health HIV Drug Resistance Database but is not publicly accessible due to patient confidentiality policies.,The data is mentioned to be not publicly accessible due to patient confidentiality.
40779404,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples.","Historical RNA or proviral DNA genotyping reports for protease (PR), RT, and integrase (IN), from local laboratories or commercial sites were collected if available. Retrospective proviral DNA genotyping of PR/RT/IN using GenoSure Archive (Monogram Biosciences, CA), which uses NGS technology was performed on BL whole blood samples.","The paper describes the collection and analysis of genotypic data from patient samples, specifically mentioning proviral DNA sequencing used for genotyping."
40779404,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data.",No evidence,"The main focus of the paper is on the clinical outcomes of HIV-1 infected participants receiving B/F/TAF, particularly those with preexisting K65R/N mutations, not on in vitro drug susceptibility testing."
40779404,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.,No evidence,The paper focuses on clinical outcomes and genotypic data but does not mention specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequences.
40779404,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"The paper reports sequencing of the protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN) genes.","Retrospective proviral DNA genotyping of PR/RT/IN using GenoSure Archive (Monogram Biosciences, CA), which uses NGS technology was performed on BL whole blood samples.","The genotypic analyses included sequencing of the PR and RT genes, and there is mention of IN sequencing in the context of resistance mutations."
40779404,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not specify the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained.,No evidence,The focus is on the clinical trial settings and participant outcomes rather than the specific geographical origins of the samples.
40779404,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not specify the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained.,No evidence,"The clinical trials mentioned span between 2016-2023, but specific years for the samples are not provided."
40779404,7,What method was used for sequencing?,"The GenoSure Archive (Monogram Biosciences, CA) method using NGS technology was used for sequencing.","Retrospective proviral DNA genotyping of PR/RT/IN using GenoSure Archive (Monogram Biosciences, CA), which uses NGS technology, was performed on BL whole blood samples.","The paper mentions the use of GenoSure Archive, which employs next-generation sequencing (NGS) technology."
40779404,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,The paper does not mention cloning of samples prior to sequencing.,No evidence,"The focus on methods does not include any mention of cloning of samples, which is typically not required for next-generation sequencing."
40779404,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Whole blood samples were sequenced,"Retrospective proviral DNA genotyping of PR/RT/IN using GenoSure Archive (Monogram Biosciences, CA), which uses NGS technology was performed on BL whole blood samples.",The paper specifies the use of whole blood samples for genotypic analysis.
40779404,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"No, all participants with preexisting K65R/N receiving B/F/TAF had virologic suppression","HIV-1 viral loads were measured by COBAS TaqMan HIV-1 Test Version 2.0 (Roche Molecular Systems, Inc). Virologic outcomes were defined using the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) Snapshot algorithm at Week 48 for participants who received B/F/TAF in the randomization phase, and by last on-treatment observation carried forward (LOCF) method through end of study (EOS) for participants randomized to B/F/TAF or switched to B/F/TAF from a comparator regimen. Virologic outcomes were defined as success (HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL) or failure (≥50 copies/mL). All participants with preexisting K65R/N receiving B/F/TAF (n = 20/20) had virologic suppression (HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL) through end of study, with no virologic failure or treatment-emergent resistance to B/F/TAF.",The study focused on individuals with preexisting K65R/N mutations who achieved virologic suppression while on B/F/TAF treatment.
40779404,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"Yes, the patients were part of Phase 2/3/3b clinical trials for B/F/TAF","The study involved post hoc analysis of participants from 6 Phase 2/3/3b clinical trials evaluating B/F/TAF during 2016-2023 (NCT02881320, NCT02603120, NCT02603107, NCT03110380, NCT03405935, NCT03631732).",The paper clearly states that the study participants were part of multiple clinical trials evaluating the efficacy of B/F/TAF.
40779404,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,2028 individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing.,"From the 2182 participants who received B/F/TAF, 2028 participants had BL genotypic RT data [1474 (n = 95), 1844 (n = 522), 1878 (n = 524), 4030 (n = 333), 4449 (n = 84), and 4580 (n = 470)]",The paper states that 2028 participants had baseline genotypic RT data available for analysis.
40779404,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"Yes, the paper reports sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.",All participants with preexisting K65R/N were VS at study start with median time on ART before study entry of 9.2 years (range 1.6–26.3 years).,"The study involved individuals who were virologically suppressed and had a history of ARV drug treatment, including those with preexisting K65R/N mutations."
40779404,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, INSTI","Before enrollment, participants had been treated with TFV (80.0%), INSTIs (50.0%), non-nucleoside RT inhibitors (NNRTI; 45.0%), and PR inhibitors (PI; 75.0%).",Participants had a history of treatment with multiple drug classes prior to the study.
40779404,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Individuals received Tenofovir, INSTIs, NNRTI, and PI.","Before enrollment, participants had been treated with TFV (80.0%), INSTIs (50.0%), non-nucleoside RT inhibitors (NNRTI; 45.0%), and PR inhibitors (PI; 75.0%).","The paper specifies the receipt of Tenofovir (TFV), INSTIs, NNRTIs, and PIs by participants before sample sequencing."
40779404,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,"Yes, sequences were made publicly available.","The present analysis leveraged historical RT genotypic data and BL proviral DNA genotypic data, including RAMs described in Table 1 and in this paper, from 6 Phase 2/3/3b B/F/TAF studies. The data of 4/20 participants with preexisting K65R/N detected by historical RNA sequencing (1844, 1878, and 4030) or retrospective proviral DNA sequencing (4030) are newly reported in this paper. Genotypic data for 16 additional participants with preexisting K65R/N were collected, previously reported, or publicly available through 6 Phase 2/3/3b B/F/TAF study clinical trial registries [NCT02881320, NCT02603120, NCT02603107, NCT03110380, NCT03405935, NCT03631732].","The paper mentions that some genotypic data were previously reported or publicly available, specifically through clinical trial registries."
40839365,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase.","The paper mentions that genotypic analysis was done on samples from individuals with confirmed virologic failure, indicating that HIV sequences were reported from patient samples."
40839365,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods: The survey was conducted between July 2022 and February 2023. We randomly selected 18 from 25 highest volume ART clinics in Malawi. We enrolled consenting adults  (maximum age is 65 years old)  on DBR for 90 months, when returning for follow-up VL testing after a previous high VL (maximum viral load is 10000000 copies/mL) and completing IAC. Enrolled PLHIV submitted plasma samples for VL testing and those with VL (least 1000 copies/mL) (confirmed virologic failure) underwent genotypic HIVDR testing. Stanford University HIVDR Database (version 9.5) was used to interpret results. We present weighted prevalence of clinically significant HIV drug resistance (Stanford levels 3-5), accounting for correlation within clinics. Results: Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase. Prevalence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) to nucleotide reverse-transcriptase inhibitors was 22.6% (95% CI: 17.4% to 27.8%), to non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors 55.1% (95% CI 48.2% to 62.1%), and to protease inhibitors 4.9% (95% CI 1.3% to 8.5%). Prevalence of major dolutegravir DRMs was 8.5% (95% CI 5.3% to 11.9%). DTG DRMs identified included G118R (8), E138K (8), R263K/A (5), Q148R (3), and T66A/I/V (3). Conclusions:  Among adult Malawians on DBRs with confirmed virologic failure, 1-in-12 had dolutegravir resistance. Adherence interventions likely help achieve resuppression, making protease inhibitor-based regimen switch unnecessary for most patients with high VL. Key Words: prevalence, dolutegravir, HIV drug resistance, Malawi (J Acquir Immune Defic Syndr 2025;100:364-370)",The study focuses on genotypic resistance and does not include in vitro susceptibility assays for the drugs tested.
40839365,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
40839365,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase.","Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase. Methods: HIV RNA was extracted using NUCLISENS easyMAG (BioMerieux) from 200uL of plasma, with 10uL of extracted RNA; gene fragments of protease and reverse transcriptase and integrase were amplified and sequenced using HIV-1 Genotyping kit with Integrase and the 3500XL Genetic Analyzers (Applied Biosystems).","The paper specifies that the genotypic analysis included the protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase regions."
40839365,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Malawi,A cross-sectional design was used to assess drug resistance among adult PLHIV with virologic nonsuppression in Malawi between July 2022 and February 2023. HIV Drug Resistance to Dolutegravir Among Adults Investigated for Antiretroviral Treatment Failure in Malawi: A 2023 National Cross-Sectional Survey,The samples were obtained from adult PLHIV in Malawi as part of the national drug resistance survey.
40839365,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022-2023,The survey was conducted between July 2022 and February 2023. We conducted a national HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey in adults on DBR with high viral load (VL) results after intensive adherence counseling (IAC).,"The paper mentions the survey conducted in 2022-2023, indicating the years from which the samples were obtained."
40839365,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"HIV RNA was extracted using NUCLISENS easyMAG (BioMerieux) from 200uL of plasma, with 10uL of extracted RNA; gene fragments of protease and reverse transcriptase and integrase were amplified and sequenced using HIV-1 Genotyping kit with Integrase and the 3500XL Genetic Analyzers (Applied Biosystems). Sanger sequence electropherograms were analyzed by using RECall (v2.32)—Web-based sequence analysis software (https://recall.bccfe.ca/account/login).","The paper describes the use of HIV-1 Genotyping kit with Integrase and the 3500XL Genetic Analyzers, which are indicative of Sanger sequencing methods."
40839365,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIV RNA was extracted using NUCLISENS easyMAG (BioMerieux) from 200uL of plasma, with 10uL of extracted RNA; gene fragments of protease and reverse transcriptase and integrase were amplified and sequenced using HIV-1 Genotyping kit with Integrase and the 3500XL Genetic Analyzers (Applied Biosystems). Sanger sequence electropherograms were analyzed by using RECall",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40839365,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,Enrolled PLHIV submitted plasma samples for VL testing and those with VL (least 1000 copies/mL) (confirmed virologic failure) underwent genotypic HIVDR testing. Plasma was separated from the venous whole blood samples that were collected in an EDTA tube by centrifugation and aliquoted at each health facility and kept at -20C until they were transported on dry ice to the National HIV Reference Laboratory (NHRL) in Lilongwe.,The genotypic analysis was performed on plasma samples from participants with confirmed virologic failure.
40839365,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase. Results:  In our first national survey of adult PLHIV with confirmed virologic failure on DBRs in Malawi, approximately 80% of the participants resuppressed their VL after IAC. Among individuals with confirmed virologic failure, prevalence of drug resistance to DTG was 8.5% .","The study explicitly mentions that genotypic analysis was done on samples from individuals with confirmed virologic failure, indicating that some sequences were obtained from such individuals."
40839365,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"The survey was conducted between July 2022 and February 2023. We randomly selected 18 from 25 highest volume ART clinics in Malawi. We enrolled consenting adults (maximum age is 65 years old) on DBR for 90 months, when returning for follow-up VL testing after a previous high VL (maximum viral load is 10000000 copies/mL) and completing IAC. A cross-sectional design was used to assess drug resistance among adult PLHIV with virologic nonsuppression in Malawi between July 2022 and February 2023. Eligible participants were PLHIV aged 15 years or older, on a DBR for at least 9 months, latest VL was 1000 copies/mL, and had completed at least 1 session of IAC per national guidelines.",The paper describes a national cross-sectional survey and does not mention any clinical trial involvement.
40839365,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,212,"Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase.  In our first national survey of adult PLHIV with confirmed virologic failure on DBRs in Malawi, approximately 80% of the participants resuppressed their VL after IAC. Among individuals with confirmed virologic failure, prevalence of drug resistance to DTG was 8.5% .",The paper states that 212 samples were successfully genotyped from individuals with confirmed virologic failure.
40839365,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase.  In our first national survey of adult PLHIV with confirmed virologic failure on DBRs in Malawi, approximately 80% of the participants resuppressed their VL after IAC. Among individuals with confirmed virologic failure, prevalence of drug resistance to DTG was 8.5% .","The paper mentions that participants were on dolutegravir-based regimens and some had virological failure, indicating they had received ARV drugs."
40839365,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs.","Among individuals with confirmed virologic failure, prevalence of drug resistance to DTG was 8.5% . Prevalence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) to nucleotide reverse-transcriptase inhibitors was 22.6% (95% CI: 17.4% to 27.8%), to non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors 55.1% (95% CI 48.2% to 62.1%), and to protease inhibitors 4.9% (95% CI 1.3% to 8.5%).","The study included individuals on dolutegravir-based regimens, which means they received NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs before sample sequencing."
40839365,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"The drugs received were NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs.","Among individuals with confirmed virologic failure, prevalence of drug resistance to DTG was 8.5% . Prevalence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) to nucleotide reverse-transcriptase inhibitors was 22.6% (95% CI: 17.4% to 27.8%), to non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors 55.1% (95% CI 48.2% to 62.1%), and to protease inhibitors 4.9% (95% CI 1.3% to 8.5%).","The study mentioned that individuals were on dolutegravir-based regimens, and some had virological failure, indicating they had received various ARV drugs."
40839365,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not mention whether the sequences were made publicly available.
40857111,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples.","""Methods: After the 96-week randomized phase of ALLIANCE, participants could enter a 48-week open-label B/F/TAF-only phase. This subanalysis reports HIV-1 resistance results to the end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF, n = 122) and to study end (B/F/TAF, n = 119; DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF, n = 89). Baseline resistance was determined using historical and screening genotypes of protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN), if available. Participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing of PR/RT/IN; those subsequently not achieving virologic suppression (VS; HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL) were included in final resistance analysis population (RAP).""","The paper discusses resistance mutations and genotypes, indicating that HIV sequences were analyzed from patient samples."
40857111,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data.","""This subanalysis reports HIV-1 resistance results to the end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF, n = 122) and to study end (B/F/TAF, n = 119; DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF, n = 89). Baseline resistance was determined using historical and screening genotypes of protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN), if available. Participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing of PR/RT/IN; those subsequently not achieving virologic suppression (VS; HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL) were included in final resistance analysis population (RAP).""",The methods section focuses on genotypic resistance testing rather than in vitro susceptibility assays.
40857111,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.,No evidence,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
40857111,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Genes sequenced include protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN).","""Methods: Baseline resistance was determined using historical and screening genotypes of protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN), if available. Participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing of PR/RT/IN; those subsequently not achieving virologic suppression (VS; HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL) were included in final resistance analysis population (RAP).""","The methods section specifies that genotypes of PR, RT, and IN were analyzed."
40857111,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The samples were obtained from a multinational population in the ALLIANCE study.,"""A strength of this analysis was that it was performed in a large multinational population and is, to our knowledge, the first randomized trial of antiretroviral therapy in an HIV/HBV treatment-naive population.""",The paper states that the study was conducted in a large multinational population.
40857111,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not specify the years from which the sequenced samples were obtained.,No evidence,The paper does not provide specific years regarding sample collection.
40857111,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The paper does not specify the sequencing method used.,No evidence,The paper does not mention the specific sequencing method employed.
40857111,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,The paper does not mention whether samples were cloned prior to sequencing.,No evidence,The paper does not provide information on whether samples were cloned.
40857111,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,The paper does not specify the type of clinical specimens that were sequenced.,No evidence,The paper does not provide specific information on the type of clinical specimens sequenced.
40857111,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"Yes, sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure.","""Criteria for final RAP inclusion were met by 7, 6, and 1 participant in the DTG+F/TDF, B/F/TAF, and DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF groups, respectively. For participants with genotyping data, no treatment-emergent primary resistance mutations to study drugs developed. From 8 participants who had resistance testing but did not qualify for inclusion in final RAP, 1 participant on DTG + F / TDF with documented nonadherence had emergent resistance mutations to tenofovir and emtricitabine and subsequently achieved VS.""",The paper mentions that participants with virological failure had postbaseline resistance testing.
40857111,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"Yes, the patients were part of the ALLIANCE clinical trial.","""The design of the phase 3 randomized ALLIANCE study (GS-US-380-4458; NCT03547908) has been described previously.""",The paper refers to the ALLIANCE study as a phase 3 randomized clinical trial.
40857111,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,"A total of 22 participants had resistance testing, and genotypic data were available for 19 participants.","""In total, 22 participants met the criteria for postbaseline HIV-1 resistance testing, and data were available for ≥1 gene for 19 (86%) of these participants, including 10 participants in the DTG+F/TDF group, 10 participants in the B/F/TAF group, and 2 participants in the DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF group.""",The paper states that 22 participants had resistance testing and genotypic data were available for 19 participants.
40857111,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"Yes, the paper reports sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.",Participants could enter a 48-week open-label B/F/TAF-only phase.,"The clinical trial involved individuals with HIV-1 who received ARV drugs, as mentioned in the methods section."
40857111,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Individuals received integrase inhibitors, NRTIs, and NNRTIs before sample sequencing.",Background: ALLIANCE is a phase 3 randomized study of bibtegravir/emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide (B/F/TAF) versus dolutegravir + emtricitabine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (DTG+F/TDF) in treatment-naive people with HIV-1/hepatitis B virus (HBV) coinfection.,"The background mentions that participants received B/F/TAF and DTG+F/TDF, which include integrase inhibitors, NRTIs, and NNRTIs."
40857111,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Individuals received bictegravir, emtricitabine, tenofovir alafenamide, dolutegravir, and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate before sample sequencing.",Background: ALLIANCE is a phase 3 randomized study of bibtegravir/emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide (B/F/TAF) versus dolutegravir + emtricitabine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (DTG+F/TDF) in treatment-naive people with HIV-1/hepatitis B virus (HBV) coinfection.,The background lists the specific drugs administered to participants.
40857111,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,The paper does not mention whether the sequences were made publicly available.,No evidence,The paper does not provide information on the public availability of the sequences.
40872801,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from patient samples. It mentions that 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed and discusses the prevalence of drug resistance mutations in these patients.","The emergence and spread of HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) pose a threat to current and future treatment options. To inform policy, this review aimed to determine the magnitude and patterns of DRMs in patients on ART in Tanzania. A systematic literature search was conducted in MEDLINE through PubMed, Embase, and CINAHL up to December 2024. A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed. The prevalence of virological failure in studies that used a threshold of >1000 and >400 copies/mL was 24.83% (95% CI: 17.85-32.53%) and 36.94% (95% CI: 24.79-50.00%), respectively. Major DRMs were observed at 87.61% (95% CI: 76.25-95.91%). A decrease in prevalence was observed in studies conducted from 2019, with a pooled prevalence of 62.15% (95% CI: 31.57-88.33%). The most frequently observed HIV-1 subtypes were subtype C at 36.20% (95% CI: 30.71-41.85%), A1 at 33.13% (95% CI: 28.23-38.20%), and subtype D at 16.00% (95% CI: 11.41-21.12%), while recombinant forms of the virus were observed at 13.29% (95% CI: 9.79-17.17%). The prevalence of DRMs against NRTIs and NNRTIs was significantly high, while that against INSTIs and PIs was low, supporting the continued use of PI- and INSTI-based regimens in Tanzania and the need for continued surveillance of DRMs.","The evidence indicates that the paper discusses the analysis of HIV sequences from patient samples, focusing on drug resistance mutations and subtype distributions."
40872801,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data.","The emergence and spread of HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) pose a threat to current and future treatment options. To inform policy, this review aimed to determine the magnitude and patterns of DRMs in patients on ART in Tanzania. A systematic literature search was conducted in MEDLINE through PubMed, Embase, and CINAHL up to December 2024. A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed. The prevalence of virological failure in studies that used a threshold of >1000 and >400 copies/mL was 24.83% (95% CI: 17.85-32.53%) and 36.94% (95% CI: 24.79-50.00%), respectively. Major DRMs were observed at 87.61% (95% CI: 76.25-95.91%). A decrease in prevalence was observed in studies conducted from 2019, with a pooled prevalence of 62.15% (95% CI: 31.57-88.33%). The most frequently observed HIV-1 subtypes were subtype C at 36.20% (95% CI: 30.71-41.85%), A1 at 33.13% (95% CI: 28.23-38.20%), and subtype D at 16.00% (95% CI: 11.41-21.12%), while recombinant forms of the virus were observed at 13.29% (95% CI: 9.79-17.17%). The prevalence of DRMs against NRTIs and NNRTIs was significantly high, while that against INSTIs and PIs was low, supporting the continued use of PI- and INSTI-based regimens in Tanzania and the need for continued surveillance of DRMs.",The evidence indicates that the paper discusses the prevalence of drug resistance mutations but does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing.
40872801,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,,,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
40872801,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,,,The paper does not provide specific details about the HIV genes that were sequenced.
40872801,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"The paper specifically focuses on studies conducted in Tanzania, so the sequenced samples were obtained from Tanzania.","The emergence and spread of HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) pose a threat to current and future treatment options. To inform policy, this review aimed to determine the magnitude and patterns of DRMs in patients on ART in Tanzania. A systematic literature search was conducted in MEDLINE through PubMed, Embase, and CINAHL up to December 2024. A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed.","The evidence indicates that the systematic review included studies conducted in Tanzania, focusing on HIV patients receiving ART."
40872801,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,"The paper reports studies conducted up to the end of December 2024, so the sequenced samples were obtained from various years leading up to 2024.","A systematic literature search was conducted in MEDLINE through PubMed, Embase, and CINAHL up to December 2024. A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed. Among the 12 studies that were excluded, 1 was a case report, 8 studies were conducted in ART naïve individuals, 2 were method validation studies, and the last one was a conference abstract whose main research article had been included. Twenty-three (23) studies were included in this review; two studies [8,16] reported outcomes separately for adults and children, making 25 studies during the analysis.","The evidence indicates that the systematic review included studies conducted up to the end of December 2024, which means the samples were obtained from various years leading up to 2024."
40872801,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The paper does not specify the method used for sequencing.,,The paper does not provide specific details about the sequencing methods used in the included studies.
40872801,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,"No, the paper does not mention that samples were cloned prior to sequencing.","The emergence and spread of HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) pose a threat to current and future treatment options. To inform policy, this review aimed to determine the magnitude and patterns of DRMs in patients on ART in Tanzania. A systematic literature search was conducted in MEDLINE through PubMed, Embase, and CINAHL up to December 2024. A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed. The prevalence of virological failure in studies that used a threshold of >1000 and >400 copies/mL was 24.83% (95% CI: 17.85-32.53%) and 36.94% (95% CI: 24.79-50.00%), respectively. Major DRMs were observed at 87.61% (95% CI: 76.25-95.91%). A decrease in prevalence was observed in studies conducted from 2019, with a pooled prevalence of 62.15% (95% CI: 31.57-88.33%). The most frequently observed HIV-1 subtypes were subtype C at 36.20% (95% CI: 30.71-41.85%), A1 at 33.13% (95% CI: 28.23-38.20%), and subtype D at 16.00% (95% CI: 11.41-21.12%), while recombinant forms of the virus were observed at 13.29% (95% CI: 9.79-17.17%). The prevalence of DRMs against NRTIs and NNRTIs was significantly high, while that against INSTIs and PIs was low, supporting the continued use of PI- and INSTI-based regimens in Tanzania and the need for continued surveillance of DRMs.",The evidence indicates that the paper discusses the analysis of drug resistance mutations without mentioning any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40872801,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,"The paper does not specify the type of clinical samples that were sequenced. However, it mentions the analysis of HIV sequences from patient samples.","The emergence and spread of HIV drug resistance mutations (DRMs) pose a threat to current and future treatment options. To inform policy, this review aimed to determine the magnitude and patterns of DRMs in patients on ART in Tanzania. A systematic literature search was conducted in MEDLINE through PubMed, Embase, and CINAHL up to December 2024. A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed. The prevalence of virological failure in studies that used a threshold of >1000 and >400 copies/mL was 24.83% (95% CI: 17.85-32.53%) and 36.94% (95% CI: 24.79-50.00%), respectively. Major DRMs were observed at 87.61% (95% CI: 76.25-95.91%). A decrease in prevalence was observed in studies conducted from 2019, with a pooled prevalence of 62.15% (95% CI: 31.57-88.33%). The most frequently observed HIV-1 subtypes were subtype C at 36.20% (95% CI: 30.71-41.85%), A1 at 33.13% (95% CI: 28.23-38.20%), and subtype D at 16.00% (95% CI: 11.41-21.12%), while recombinant forms of the virus were observed at 13.29% (95% CI: 9.79-17.17%). The prevalence of DRMs against NRTIs and NNRTIs was significantly high, while that against INSTIs and PIs was low, supporting the continued use of PI- and INSTI-based regimens in Tanzania and the need for continued surveillance of DRMs.","The evidence indicates that the paper discusses the analysis of HIV sequences from patient samples, focusing on drug resistance mutations and subtype distributions."
40872801,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"Yes, the paper reports sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen.","The primary outcome of this study was the magnitude and patterns of drug resistance mutations among PLHIV receiving ART, while the secondary outcome was the HIV-1 subtypes' distribution in the included studies. Some studies followed participants longitudinally, assessing virological failure as well as DRMs prevalence, and other studies enrolled participants who already had virological failure. We are reporting virological failure in studies in which the outcome was assessed. All the results from our analysis are summarized in Table 2, while individual outcomes are fully described in their respective sub-sections.",The evidence indicates that the paper includes studies that assessed virological failure and reported sequences from individuals with virological failure.
40872801,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,The paper does not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial.,"A systematic literature review was conducted from February to May 2025 following PRISMA standards, including original studies that were conducted in Tanzania involving participants receiving ART published in English by the end of December 2024. The systematic review protocol was registered in the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (PROSPERO) on 7 January 2025 under the registration number CRD42025606698.",The evidence indicates that the paper included original studies conducted in Tanzania and did not mention that the patients were part of a clinical trial.
40872801,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,"The paper mentions that 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed for drug resistance mutations, but it does not specify the exact number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing.","A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed. The primary outcome of this study was the magnitude and patterns of drug resistance mutations among PLHIV receiving ART, while the secondary outcome was the HIV-1 subtypes' distribution in the included studies.","The evidence indicates that 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed, but it does not specify the exact number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing."
40872801,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,"Yes, the paper reports HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.","A total of 9685 HIV patients from 23 eligible studies were analyzed. The primary outcome of this study was the magnitude and patterns of drug resistance mutations among PLHIV receiving ART, while the secondary outcome was the HIV-1 subtypes' distribution in the included studies. Some studies followed participants longitudinally, assessing virological failure as well as DRMs prevalence, and other studies enrolled participants who already had virological failure. We are reporting virological failure in studies in which the outcome was assessed. All the results from our analysis are summarized in Table 2, while individual outcomes are fully described in their respective sub-sections.",The evidence indicates that the paper includes studies that reported sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.
40872801,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"The paper mentions that the studies included individuals receiving ART, which includes NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs.","A systematic literature review was conducted from February to May 2025 following PRISMA standards, including original studies that were conducted in Tanzania involving participants receiving ART published in English by the end of December 2024. The systematic review protocol was registered in the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (PROSPERO) on 7 January 2025 under the registration number CRD42025606698.",The evidence indicates that the paper included studies that reported sequences from individuals who had previously received various drug classes before sample sequencing.
40872801,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,,,The paper does not specify the exact drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing.
40872801,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,"The paper mentions that data supporting the findings are available from the corresponding author upon request, but it does not specify if the sequences were made publicly available.",Data supporting the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon request.,The paper states that data supporting the findings are available upon request but does not confirm public availability.
40938120,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"A total of 1,048 eligible pol sequences were analyzed.",The paper discusses the analysis of HIV pol gene sequences from patient samples.
40938120,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database was used to identify PDR levels and mutations, no in vitro data",The study focuses on genotypic resistance analysis without mentioning in vitro assays.
40938120,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,PX133190-PX134237,HIV-1 sequence data generated in this study have been deposited in GenBank and are available under accession numbers PX133190-PX134237.,The paper explicitly lists the GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
40938120,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,Pol gene,"An in-house RT nested PCR was used to amplify a 1,061 bp fragment of the pol gene, covering the full-length protease (PR, 99 codons) and the first 299 codons of the RT gene.","The study mentions sequencing the pol gene, including regions for protease and reverse transcriptase."
40938120,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,China,"This study assessed the prevalence, associated factors, and transmission networks of PDR among newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals in Guangxi, China, from 2019 to 2022.","The paper is focused on a study conducted in China, specifically Guangxi."
40938120,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2019 to 2022,"A cross-sectional study was conducted between 2019 and 2022 in Nanning, Guangxi, involving 1,260 newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals with HIV.",The study covers a period from 2019 to 2022.
40938120,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"PCR products were confirmed by agarose gel electrophoresis, and positive amplicons were sequenced by Sangon Biotech (Shanghai, China) using the Applied Biosystems 3730XL Genetic Analyzer.",The paper describes Sanger sequencing method used for clinical samples.
40938120,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"PCR products were confirmed by agarose gel electrophoresis, and positive amplicons were sequenced by Sangon Biotech (Shanghai, China) using the Applied Biosystems 3730XL Genetic Analyzer.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40938120,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,Venous blood samples (10 mL) were collected from each participant. Plasma was separated by centrifugation and stored in aliquots at −80°C for further analysis.,The study involved sequencing HIV RNA extracted from plasma samples.
40938120,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"We included newly diagnosed HIV-infected individuals who were ART-naive. Laboratory testing: HIV-1 RNA was extracted from plasma samples using the High Pure Viral RNA Kit (Roche, Germany).","The study involved ART-naive individuals, so no virological failure sequences were reported."
40938120,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"A cross-sectional survey using a convenience sampling method was conducted across the nine counties and districts of Nanning City, Guangxi Province, from 1 January 2019 to 31 December 2022. During each survey period, staff at the recruitment sites recruited individuals newly diagnosed with HIV. To construct the molecular transmission network, we recruited more than 60% newly diagnosed HIV patients each year.",The paper does not indicate that the patients were part of a clinical trial.
40938120,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,"1,260","A cross-sectional study was conducted between 2019 and 2022 in Nanning, Guangxi, involving 1,260 newly diagnosed, ART-naive individuals with HIV.","The study involved 1,260 newly diagnosed individuals from whom samples were obtained."
40938120,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,No,We included newly diagnosed HIV-infected individuals who were ART-naive.,"The study specifically included ART-naive individuals, meaning they had not previously received ARV drugs."
40938120,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"We included newly diagnosed HIV-infected individuals who were ART-naive. Laboratory testing: HIV-1 RNA was extracted from plasma samples using the High Pure Viral RNA Kit (Roche, Germany).","The study involved ART-naive individuals, meaning they had not previously received ARV drugs."
40938120,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,None,"We included newly diagnosed HIV-infected individuals who were ART-naive. Laboratory testing: HIV-1 RNA was extracted from plasma samples using the High Pure Viral RNA Kit (Roche, Germany).","The study involved ART-naive individuals, meaning they had not previously received ARV drugs."
40938120,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,HIV-1 sequence data generated in this study have been deposited in GenBank and are available under accession numbers PX133190-PX134237.,The paper explicitly states that the sequences have been deposited in GenBank.
40938996,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"No, the paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples.","No, the paper does not report HIV sequences from patient samples.","The study focuses on laboratory-generated virus samples and their mutations in response to antiretroviral therapy, rather than patient-derived sequences."
40938996,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"Yes, the paper reports in vitro drug susceptibility data.","We first examined the replication of the NC mutants in the SupT1 T cell line; we performed single-cycle DTG sensitivity assays using a vesicular stomatitis virus G (VSV-G)-pseudotyped HIV-1 nanoLuc reporter virus; SupT1 T cells were incubated with RT-normalized VSV-G-pseudotyped nanoLuc reporter HIV-1 in the presence of varying concentrations of DTG; SupT1  cells were exposed to Env(-), DNase I-treated, VSV-G-pseudotyped WT or NC-G19S virus particles; TZM-bl cells were incubated with RT-normalized virus stocks.","The paper includes multiple in vitro assays that measure the sensitivity of HIV-1 mutants to dolutegravir (DTG) and other antiretroviral drugs, indicating that in vitro drug susceptibility data are reported."
40938996,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide direct GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates.,No evidence,"The paper does not mention specific GenBank accession numbers for the HIV isolates used in the study, as it primarily discusses laboratory-generated mutants."
40938996,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"The paper reports sequencing of the gag, pol, env, and nef genes.","At the time points indicated by the arrows, genomic DNA was extracted from the drug-treated cultures, and the Gag-Pol and Env-Nef coding regions were sequenced.",The long-term passage experiments involved sequencing the Gag-Pol and Env-Nef coding regions to identify mutations.
40938996,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not report specific countries from which sequenced samples were obtained.,No evidence,"The paper does not mention the geographic origin of patient samples, focusing instead on laboratory-generated viruses."
40938996,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The paper does not specify the years from which sequenced samples were obtained.,No evidence,"The paper does not provide a timeline for the collection of sequenced samples, as it does not report patient-derived data."
40938996,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The paper used Sanger sequencing for genomic DNA extraction.,"The first PCR product was diluted 10-fold and used as a template for measuring R/U5 DNA using M667 primer, AA55 primer, and HIV-FAM probe; Twenty-four hours postinfection with VSV-G-pseudotyped viruses, total DNA from the infected SupT1 T cell line was extracted as described above. The 2-LTR circle junction was amplified using PrimeSTAR GXL DNA polymerase (Takara Bio) with the following primers; The first PCR was performed using PrimeSTAR GXL DNA polymerase (Takara Bio) with Alu-HIV and M661 primers. The first PCR cycling conditions were as follows: initial denaturation (98°C, 10s), 22 cycles of denaturation (98°C, 10s), annealing (60°C, 15s), and extension (68°C, 10min) The first PCR product was diluted 10-fold and used as a template for measuring R/U5 DNA using M667 primer,AA55 primer, and HIV-FAM probe; sequencing analysis across the HIV-1 genome. ; A series of pNL4-3 NC mutants was described previously. We previously reported that mutations in the viral nucleocapsid (NC) are selected in the presence of the INSTI dolutegravir (DTG). Here, we show that these NC mutations accelerate the kinetics of viral DNA integration, suggesting that they limit the window of time available for INSTIs to block viral DNA integration; we performed single-cycle DTG sensitivity assays using a vesicular stomatitis virus G (VSV-G)-pseudotyped HIV-1 nanoLuc reporter virus; we first examined the replication of the NC mutants in the SupT1 T cell line;  pNL4-3/M661 KFS HIV-1 molecular clone containing  the Alu PCR integration CS regions were sent for sequencing. pNL4-3/NL LTR Red .6ATRi KFS HIV-1-based  vector containing the U5 and R region after the entry portion of HIV-1 were made for phosphorylation of reverse transcriptase used are from previously described pNL4-3/NL LTR .6ATRi KFS .cross shuffling  D112N, E118S, G123E, L204R, F245L, T347A, D348A, D349A R ending linker region; Mescence  C37S, H45R, C61A, M62R, H86P, D90Y; D70N to Max 62 effect of previous generation drugs assay used. C subunit f حامل ر س و 4 (سة و، CDB3. The second PR icycle Internal HEX fluorescent probe, 5′-TGTGAC TCGATGCTCTG grille – 3’, was used. The second PCR was performed using 1 𝓵 PCR purified products as templates with internal probe. Sample in 20 𝓵 reaction assay thermocycler under the following condition: 95 𝒉 10s, 95 𝒉 10s, 40 cycles of 95 𝒉 1s, 60 𝒉 30s, 65 𝒉 30s.","The paper describes the use of Sanger sequencing to confirm the sequences of PCR products, indicating that this method was employed for sequencing."
40938996,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,"Yes, samples were cloned prior to sequencing.",The PCR amplicon was purified and cloned into pCR-Blunt II-TOPO vector (Thermo Fisher Scientific) per the manufacturer's instructions.,The paper mentions that PCR amplicons were cloned into a TOPO vector prior to sequencing.
40938996,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,The paper sequenced laboratory-generated HIV-1 mutant samples in vitro.,"In this study, we characterized a panel of NC mutants selected in the presence of DTG.","The paper focuses on the sequencing of laboratory-generated viral samples, not patient-derived clinical specimens."
40938996,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"No, the paper does not report sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen.","Our previous study demonstrated that HIV-1 frequently acquires NC mutations in the presence of DTG independent of viral strain; HIV-1 acquires mutations in Env, NC, and IN in the presence of DTG in primary T cells. These selected NC mutations reduce sensitivity to INSTIs but not to other classes of antiretrovirals, indicating that the resistance conferred by NC mutations is specific to INSTIs.","The focus of the paper is on laboratory studies and selected viral mutants, not on clinical patient data."
40938996,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,The paper involvement  patients in a clinical trial.,"the 4109041 study was a Phase II, randomized, multicenter, open-label clinical trial conducted across eight African study sites.","The paper focun on the result of clinical trial, and enrolled  patients."
40938996,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,The paper does not specify how many cases had sequencing obtained.,,The paper does not mention the number of individual patient samples obtained for sequencing.
40938996,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,The paper does not report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs.,No evidence,"The study focuses on laboratory-generated virus samples and their mutations in response to antiretroviral therapy, rather than patient-derived sequences."
